# wrapped_zero_region_drop.tkr — the COL-F0c wrapped-refcount fixture (crumb 0023). `main.tks`
# loops N times; each iteration creates one object as its own region, retains it once, releases it
# once (driving the count straight to zero), and immediately, in that SAME iteration, allocates a
# fresh region — counting a "freed" token whenever the fresh region recycles the just-dropped
# header. Since the LIFO header free-list only returns an address once it has been pushed onto it,
# a token counted in the SAME iteration proves the drop happened ON THE SPOT, not deferred to the
# loop's or the program's end. Exits 0 only if every one of the N objects was freed exactly then.
# The compiler holds no `wrapped` objects, so this path is not fixpoint-exercised. Pinned to the C
# backend.

Feature: wrapped_zero_region_drop — the last release drops the object's region immediately, not deferred

  Scenario: each_loop_iterations_last_release_frees_its_object_before_the_next_iteration
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
